window.docContents[211].resolve({"/Slang/#Hoare-Logic--Slang___-Arithmetic-and-Boolean-Expressions--Evaluation-as-a-Relation--Inference-Rule-Notation":{"contents":"\n\nThe first two quizzes here seem kind of boring.\n\nWhich rules are needed to prove the following?.mult (.plus (.num 3) (.num 1)) (.num 0) ⇓ 0\n(A) num and plus\n(B) num only\n(C) num and mult\n(D) mult and plus\n(E) num, mult, and plus\n\n(E) num, mult, and plus\n\n\n\nNot sure if we need ⇓b, or whether we can define\n⇓ overloaded. Don't understand Lean notation yet!\n\nAbout Bexp.eval below: We should discuss a way to recall definitions without\nhaving to write them out manually like this. I think a simple #print may work as an\nalternative, assuming there are no namespace issues..\n\nHere, again, is the definition of the Bexp.eval function:def Bexp.eval (b : Bexp) : Bool :=\n  match b with\n  | bool b     => b\n  | eq   a₁ a₂ => a₁.eval == a₂.eval\n  | neq  a₁ a₂ => a₁.eval != a₂.eval\n  | le   a₁ a₂ => a₁.eval ≤ a₂.eval\n  | gt   a₁ a₂ => a₁.eval > a₂.eval\n  | not  b₁    => !eval b₁\n  | and  b₁ b₂ => eval b₁ && eval b₂\nWrite out a corresponding definition of boolean evaluation as a relation\nin inference rule notation.\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Slang: Arithmetic and Boolean Expressions\u0009Evaluation as a Relation","header":"2.2.1. Inference Rule Notation","id":"/Slang/#Hoare-Logic--Slang___-Arithmetic-and-Boolean-Expressions--Evaluation-as-a-Relation--Inference-Rule-Notation"}});